  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_section_1",
      "section_title": "Oxygen cylinder and flowmeter",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The examiner expects you to identify the oxygen cylinder, explain its components, demonstrate safe oxygen delivery, interpret the gauges, calculate the remaining oxygen duration, and state the precautions."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Name: Medical oxygen cylinder with pressure regulator and flowmeter."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Classification: Medical gas storage and oxygen-delivery equipment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Principle: Oxygen is stored as a compressed gas under high pressure. A pressure regulator reduces the cylinder pressure to a usable level, while the flowmeter controls the rate of oxygen delivery in litres per minute (L/min)."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Common uses"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Emergency management of hypoxaemia.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Oxygen delivery during resuscitation and transport.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Supplemental oxygen when wall oxygen is unavailable.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Oxygen delivery in wards, clinics, ambulances and neonatal units, using appropriate equipment.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Parts and functions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Cylinder body"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stores compressed medical oxygen. Cylinders vary in size and colour according to local standards; never identify the gas by colour alone. Check the label and markings."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Pressure gauge"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Indicates the pressure remaining in the cylinder, commonly in bar or psi. It helps estimate how much oxygen remains."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It does not directly show the flow rate being delivered."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Pressure regulator"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reduces the very high cylinder pressure to a suitable working pressure for the attached equipment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Flowmeter"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Controls and displays the oxygen flow rate in L/min. Depending on the model, it may contain a floating ball or bobbin inside a graduated tube."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "5. Outlet and oxygen tubing"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Connect the flowmeter to the prescribed oxygen-delivery device, such as nasal prongs, a simple face mask or a compatible resuscitation system."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Other components may include the cylinder valve, valve-protection cap, pressure-relief mechanism and a compatible connector or key, depending on the cylinder design."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. The crucial distinction: pressure gauge vs flowmeter",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Pressure gauge",
                    "Flowmeter"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Measures",
                      "Cylinder pressure",
                      "Oxygen flow rate"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Units",
                      "bar or psi",
                      "L/min"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Main purpose",
                      "Estimates remaining supply",
                      "Sets delivery rate"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Example",
                      "100 bar",
                      "4 L/min"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE pearl: A cylinder can have a high pressure reading but deliver no oxygen if the valve is closed, the regulator is faulty or the outlet is obstructed."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. How to use the oxygen cylinder — practical demonstration",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess the patient first"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess airway, breathing, respiratory effort, mental state, SpO₂ and other vital signs. Give emergency oxygen promptly when indicated, without delaying resuscitation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Check the cylinder"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Confirm it is medical oxygen, inspect its condition, check the pressure gauge, verify the regulator is compatible and ensure sufficient oxygen is available for the expected duration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Secure and connect"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Secure the cylinder in an approved holder or trolley. Connect the correct regulator and flowmeter according to the manufacturer's instructions. Keep the valve and equipment free of oil and grease."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Open the cylinder valve safely"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Open it according to the manufacturer's instructions and check the pressure reading. Do not force a stuck valve. Check for leaks and ensure the system is functioning."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Set the prescribed flow"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Adjust the flowmeter to the prescribed rate. For a variable-area flowmeter, read the float according to its markings and manufacturer's instructions; many models are read at the centre of the float."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Apply the correct delivery device"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Fit nasal prongs, a face mask or another appropriate interface. Make sure it is correctly positioned and that tubing is not kinked or disconnected."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Monitor and document"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess SpO₂, respiratory effort, mental state and clinical response. Titrate oxygen to the appropriate target, document the device and flow rate, and monitor the remaining supply."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "When finishing: Turn off the cylinder according to local procedure, allow residual pressure to be released from the regulator if the manufacturer instructs this, return controls to the specified position and store the cylinder safely."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Oxygen flow rates and delivery devices",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These are common adult examples for OSCE revision. Actual flow must follow the device specification, clinical condition and local protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Delivery device",
                    "Typical flow rate",
                    "Key point"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Nasal cannula",
                      "1–6 L/min",
                      "Convenient for mild oxygen requirements"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Simple face mask",
                      "5–10 L/min",
                      "Generally do not use below 5 L/min because of CO₂ rebreathing risk"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Venturi mask",
                      "Device-specific, often 2–15 L/min",
                      "Delivers a specified oxygen concentration when set up correctly"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Non-rebreather mask",
                      "Usually 10–15 L/min",
                      "Inflate reservoir bag before application and keep it from collapsing substantially"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bag-valve-mask with oxygen reservoir",
                      "Commonly 15 L/min",
                      "Used during resuscitation; ensure effective ventilation and reservoir function"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: These flow rates are not interchangeable. Some devices, especially Venturi masks, have specific flow requirements printed on the adapter. Neonatal and pediatric equipment may require different settings."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Calculating how long the cylinder will last",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a favourite calculation station."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Formula:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The cylinder factor is generally related to the cylinder's water capacity in litres when pressure is measured in bar. Confirm the correct factor for the specific cylinder. Allow for a safety reserve and do not plan to use the cylinder down to zero pressure."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_8",
              "group_title": "Worked example",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A cylinder has a water capacity of 2 L, a pressure reading of 100 bar, and oxygen is running at 4 L/min."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Estimated duration before allowing for reserve"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_9",
              "group_title": "50 minutes",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Actual usable duration will be shorter if a safety reserve is maintained or the system has additional losses."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Provides a portable oxygen supply where piped oxygen is unavailable.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Useful during emergency transfers and transport.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Allows controlled oxygen flow when used with a suitable regulator and flowmeter.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can support different oxygen-delivery devices.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Does not require an electrical supply for basic cylinder delivery, although some attached equipment may require power.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages and limitations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Oxygen supply is finite; the cylinder must be replaced or refilled.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Heavy cylinders can be difficult to transport.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Incorrect regulators or connections may cause leaks or failure.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Incorrect flow settings can result in inadequate oxygen delivery or excessive oxygen exposure.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Pressure alone does not guarantee that the delivery system is functioning.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Cylinders create a fire hazard because oxygen supports combustion.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Safety precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Fire safety"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No smoking, flames or sparks. Keep oxygen away from combustible materials and sources of ignition. Oxygen itself is not flammable, but it greatly accelerates combustion."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Correct handling"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Secure the cylinder upright where required, protect the valve, avoid dropping it and use the appropriate trolley for transport."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No oil or grease"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Never apply oil, grease or petroleum-based products to oxygen valves, regulators or fittings. Use only approved equipment and accessories."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Prescribe and monitor oxygen"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Oxygen is a treatment, not a substitute for assessing and treating the cause of respiratory distress. Use an appropriate target saturation and reassess the patient."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Check supply and leaks"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect equipment before use, ensure connections are compatible and monitor the gauge during treatment. Never use a damaged cylinder or faulty regulator."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_13",
              "group_title": "Oxygen saturation targets: examiner points",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "For many acutely ill adults, a common target is SpO₂ 94–98%.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "For patients at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure, a common initial target is 88–92%, pending blood gas results and clinical review.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "In children, targets depend on the clinical condition and local pediatric guidance.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "In preterm neonates, oxygen targets are especially specific and must follow neonatal protocols.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not tell an examiner that every patient should receive oxygen at 15 L/min. The device, flow and target must match the clinical situation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Pediatric considerations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Choose an age-appropriate interface that fits without causing pressure injury or distress.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use a pediatric-sized mask or cannula when appropriate.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Monitor SpO₂, respiratory rate, work of breathing, mental state and response to treatment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check that the flowmeter and delivery device are suitable for the prescribed flow, particularly in infants.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "During neonatal resuscitation, use the recommended oxygen concentration and monitoring protocol for gestational age and clinical circumstances; do not assume 100% oxygen is always required.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "In infants and children, deterioration may be rapid. Escalate promptly if oxygenation or ventilation remains inadequate.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Mnemonic: OXYGEN",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_15",
              "group_title": "O",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Observe the patient and SpO₂."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_16",
              "group_title": "X",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "eXamine the cylinder, pressure and connections."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_17",
              "group_title": "Y",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Yield the prescribed flow using the correct device."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_18",
              "group_title": "G",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Guard against fire, leaks and unsafe handling."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_19",
              "group_title": "E",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Evaluate the response and remaining supply."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_20",
              "group_title": "N",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Note the flow rate, device and clinical response."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Common OSCE questions and model answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_21",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is the function of the pressure regulator?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It reduces the high pressure inside the oxygen cylinder to a usable working pressure for the delivery system."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the difference between the pressure gauge and flowmeter?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The pressure gauge indicates the pressure remaining in the cylinder, while the flowmeter sets and displays the oxygen flow rate in litres per minute."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Why must a simple face mask generally not be run below 5 L/min?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Low flow can permit exhaled carbon dioxide to accumulate within the mask, increasing the risk of rebreathing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. How do you calculate the remaining oxygen duration?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Multiply the cylinder pressure by its specified cylinder factor, then divide by the oxygen flow rate. Subtract an appropriate safety reserve when estimating usable time."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What are the main hazards of oxygen cylinders?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Fire intensification, high-pressure gas hazards, cylinder damage or falling, leaks, incorrect connections and interruption of oxygen supply."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What do you do if the patient remains hypoxic despite oxygen?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reassess airway, breathing and circulation; check the oxygen source, connections, flowmeter and delivery interface; escalate urgently; and treat the underlying cause. Provide ventilatory support if indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Can oxygen be prescribed at the same flow rate for every patient?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. The device, flow rate, oxygen concentration and saturation target depend on the patient’s condition and relevant clinical protocol."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. Common mistakes and examiner tips",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "oxygen_cylinder_and_flowmeter_group_22",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Confusing cylinder pressure with oxygen flow rate.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Forgetting to check the cylinder label and remaining supply.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Failing to secure the cylinder.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Using a simple face mask at an inappropriately low flow.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Forgetting that a Venturi mask has a specified flow requirement.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
